  "result": "All four tests pass.\n\n**What I did:** Added `catalog/service.py` with the `NotFound` exception and the `Catalog` class from SPEC.md. Records live in a dict keyed by id, which preserves insertion order so `list()` returns creation order for free. `get`, `update`, and `list` return copies so callers can't mutate internal state. `update` validates field names before looking up the record, so an unknown field raises `ValueError` even for a missing id.\n\n**Test result:**\n\n```\n4 passed in 0.02s\n```\n\n**Approach chosen:** Approach A, sequential integers. The spec describes a single in-memory, per-catalog store with no cross-process coordination, so the global-uniqueness and pre-insertion generation benefits of UUIDs don't apply, while sequential ids are simpler, sortable, and easier to read in tests and logs. The counter is never reset or reused after deletes, so ids stay unique and stable as the spec requires.",
